WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:19.119
Hey there, Little Fox readers! I'm reading a really suspenseful fantasy book, Dragons of Gold.

2
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.107
It's about some magical dragons that protect the kingdom of the Golden Emperor.

3
00:00:24.108 --> 00:00:30.179
Oh, that reminds me! I want to tell you about our latest landmarks lesson.

4
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:36.168
Yesterday in Mr. Fay's class, we learned about an awesome place in Beijing, China.

5
00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:45.835
The Forbidden City, which was the home of Chinese emperors for nearly five hundred years, is the largest palace complex in the world.

6
00:00:45.836 --> 00:00:54.154
And get this: the complex is decorated with thousands of cool-looking dragons, too many of them to even count!

7
00:00:54.155 --> 00:01:05.833
Construction of the Forbidden City began in 1406, when the third emperor from the Ming dynasty decided to build a new palace complex in the city of Beijing.

8
00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:14.564
According to most historical accounts, it took thousands of people working for about 14 years to build the Forbidden City.

9
00:01:14.565 --> 00:01:20.214
But what's really amazing is how the workers moved some of the building materials.

10
00:01:20.215 --> 00:01:29.715
To transport heavy blocks of stone thousands of miles, workers created ice paths by pouring water on the frozen ground.

11
00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:34.460
The slippery ice made it much easier to drag the stone blocks along.

12
00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:40.625
And then, when huge logs needed to be moved for some of the buildings, guess what the workers did . . .

13
00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:46.210
They waited for heavy rains to come and wash the logs into rivers!

14
00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:53.968
From there, boatmen could steer the logs into the Grand Canal, and then all the wood could be towed to Beijing.

15
00:01:53.992 --> 00:01:58.200
Now that's what I call a smart idea!

16
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.854
The Forbidden City was finally completed around 1420.

17
00:02:02.855 --> 00:02:13.785
When the emperor moved into the palace complex, it had over 800 buildings with 9,999 rooms! Wow!

18
00:02:13.786 --> 00:02:22.574
Chinese emperors continued living in the Forbidden City until 1912, when the Chinese Revolution ended imperial rule.

19
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:28.081
The last emperor, Puyi, was only about six years old at the time.

20
00:02:28.082 --> 00:02:35.080
And for the next 12 years, he and his family were basically imprisoned inside the Forbidden City.

21
00:02:35.081 --> 00:02:41.156
By now you're probably wondering why the palace complex is called the Forbidden City.

22
00:02:41.157 --> 00:02:47.481
Well, Mr. Fay explained that it was because most people were forbidden from entering it.

23
00:02:47.482 --> 00:02:52.477
Even members of the imperial family were restricted from some areas.

24
00:02:52.478 --> 00:03:00.287
There was only one person who could go wherever he wanted in the complex—the emperor himself!

25
00:03:00.288 --> 00:03:10.140
Still today the first thing you notice as you approach the Forbidden City are the enormous red walls—almost ten meters high—surrounding the place.

26
00:03:10.141 --> 00:03:13.716
The Meridian Gate serves as the main entrance.

27
00:03:13.717 --> 00:03:21.547
This gate has five different doors, but back in imperial times, people couldn't just walk through any door they wanted.

28
00:03:21.548 --> 00:03:24.673
Instead they had to enter based on their rank.

29
00:03:24.697 --> 00:03:33.284
The emperor's family members used the door at the west side of the gate while low-ranking ministers went through the door on the east side.

30
00:03:33.284 --> 00:03:37.185
Only the emperor entered through the middle door.

31
00:03:37.186 --> 00:03:44.128
Inside the Forbidden City there are two main areas: the Outer Court and the Inner Court.

32
00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:51.254
During imperial rule, business was conducted in the Outer Court, which has three main halls.

33
00:03:51.255 --> 00:04:02.743
The Hall of Supreme Harmony is the largest wooden building in China and with its elaborate decorations, it's probably the most magnificent structure inside the whole complex.

34
00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:12.994
In front of the building, there's a huge open space, big enough to fit everyone who lived in the Forbidden City—about 100,000 people!

35
00:04:12.995 --> 00:04:24.662
The Hall of Supreme Harmony was also where the emperors' throne sat and where they held ceremonies for things like weddings, the New Year, and sending soldiers out to war.

36
00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:28.929
Emperors lived with their families and servants inside the Inner Court.

37
00:04:28.953 --> 00:04:33.332
And by "servants," I don't mean just a housekeeper or two.

38
00:04:33.332 --> 00:04:37.316
The Chinese emperors had thousands of servants.

39
00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.055
No wonder the Forbidden City is so huge!

40
00:04:41.055 --> 00:04:48.561
Just beyond the Inner Court is the Imperial Garden with fishponds, sculptures, and a small temple.

41
00:04:48.562 --> 00:04:53.710
Mr. Fay said the garden was a place where emperors could relax and meditate.

42
00:04:53.711 --> 00:05:01.542
I wonder if they spent a lot of time out there—if you ask me, ruling a whole empire sounds pretty stressful!

43
00:05:01.543 --> 00:05:11.274
While the buildings in the Forbidden City are all scarlet in color, the roofs are lined with yellow tiles since yellow symbolized the emperor's power.

44
00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:18.862
Many of the decorations inside the palaces are yellow or gold too, including the bricks used for the floors.

45
00:05:18.863 --> 00:05:23.950
Remember what I said earlier about all the dragons inside the Forbidden City?

46
00:05:23.951 --> 00:05:33.934
Well, we found out that dragons symbolize important imperial characteristics such as benevolence, excellence, and heroism.

47
00:05:33.935 --> 00:05:37.661
That's why the Forbidden City has so many of them.

48
00:05:37.662 --> 00:05:52.159
There's an incredible "dragon pavement" with dragons carved out of stone; dragon statues perched on top of rooftops; and the Nine Dragon Screen, a really famous glazed tile screen.

49
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.923
If you ever get to see the screen, you might notice that one tile looks slightly different from the rest.

50
00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:05.071
That's because right before an emperor was going to inspect the screen, a tile broke.

51
00:06:05.072 --> 00:06:16.050
So the craftsmen, who didn't want to get in trouble, quickly made a wooden tile and stuck it in place. Luckily the emperor never noticed!

52
00:06:16.051 --> 00:06:20.404
Each year millions of tourists visit the Forbidden City.

53
00:06:20.405 --> 00:06:33.616
Many head to the Palace Museum to see artifacts from imperial China such as precious silks, jewelry, porcelain, jade, bronzes, and other valuable works of art.

54
00:06:33.617 --> 00:06:40.370
Today a lot of the buildings inside the Forbidden City are set up just as they were hundreds of years ago.

55
00:06:40.371 --> 00:06:51.619
According to Mr. Fay, that makes it easy to imagine the royal court back in the days when servants, couriers, and important government officials were bustling around.

56
00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:56.269
Well, I hope you enjoyed your quick tour of the Forbidden City.

57
00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.876
If you'll excuse me now, I'm dying to get back to my book.

58
00:07:00.877 --> 00:07:08.947
The dragons are about to rescue the emperor's son, the Golden Prince, from the evil Jade Warlock!
